<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="7f190ad9">&lt;p&gt;
    An iterative process for defect retesting must be completed and successful results should be obtained during retesting
    (testing team / business users - as applicable).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once the defect is fixed by the developer and successfully retested by the appropriate party, then the defect&apos;s status
    in the tracking tool must be updated to &quot;Resolved&quot;.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="72dd053"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="5255ca2c">&lt;p&gt;
    All identified defects should have a resolved state by the end of the test effort. However, the resolved state for each
    defect may vary. For example, many defect fixes can be addressed through code changes, requirement adjustments or even
    a combination of both. Once retested, the defect fixes and reflected in the resolved state. In other situations,
    defects may be unfixable where any code changes or requirement adjustments would cause existing code to break. These
    defects typically require decisions by leadership to determine the potential resolution or mitigation process for the
    defect. Even though the defect is not fixed, the resolved state should reflect the outcome of the defect.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="bafcf778"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="1d62b264">To verify defects in the target solution have been addressed and resolved.</attribute>
<attribute name="alternatives" nodeId="e094b3eb"></attribute>
</Element>
